<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walls of Constantinople</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">completed 413 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Theodosian Walls</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> were the fortifications protecting the Byzantine capital, above all the land walls known as the Theodosian Walls, completed in 413 CE. Their triple line of moat, outer wall and inner wall made the city almost impregnable for centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> profile treats them as central to the empire's survival, until they were breached by Ottoman artillery in 1453.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="Walls of Constantinople" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodosian_Walls_of_Constantinople.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
